#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120903} :activity/id #uuid "1cbd6cd3-5c23-5011-83d6-29ca3fbd3d32" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927 :topic/id #uuid "1f1f7b83-a1f4-5b1c-888f-a20ae593bd72" :topic/math-academy-id 1813} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121006 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}\n$$\n\nIf we try to evaluate the limit by directly substituting $x=0$, we get a confusing result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{x^{3} - 2x}{x^{2} + 4x} &= ((0)^{3} - 2(0))/((0)^{2} + 4(0)) = \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 0 0\n$$\n\nis called an **indeterminate form** because we are unable to determine the value of the limit from this expression.\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before evaluating the limit, we obtain a more clear result. To simplify the fraction, we can factor the numerator and denominator and then cancel out any common factors.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} & = \\dfrac{x(x^2-2)}{x(x+4)} \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{\\cancel{x}(x^2-2)}{\\cancel{x}(x+4)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the limit of the simplified fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} &= \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{(0)^2-2}{(0)+4} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{-2}{4} \\\\ & = -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "358e387e-1f79-5799-a6bc-d9a94fd46dd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 246 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ef3410b5-607f-5268-83e7-58a7aeef5936" :step/math-academy-id 444 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119937 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119677 :question/id #uuid "7a33db07-63f3-5f8a-b2cd-ca662b91e063" :question/math-academy-id "e-71"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2072b915-15f9-5296-96dd-707e38df3d10" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050323}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235426}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With One Quadratic Term by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "213f9703-950a-5e68-9e6e-75e4744f5178" :step/math-academy-id 445 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120911}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119938 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119680 :question/id #uuid "81aac71f-0559-51f5-9567-928d99c08c77" :question/math-academy-id "e-72"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eaa6cbd-46e1-5681-b536-06d16e8d3654" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049153}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120407}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With Higher Order Terms by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "2b405cc4-9d67-53b0-94ff-33f5133c2609" :step/math-academy-id 448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120911 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119942 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119681 :question/id #uuid "0c0f5ce6-a84b-51a8-ac60-c557966ec85c" :question/math-academy-id "e-73"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea72566-55ba-582e-802c-e5ce5ef4a9fc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120405}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With Two Quadratic Terms by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "de1bdae6-79f1-521f-90b6-f0f8755e4df7" :step/math-academy-id 451 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120905}}] :activity/title "Calculating Limits of Rational Functions by Factoring" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref